Z||000000||Fret Naught||08-23-2000||10:17 PM||||Say any of you folks active in the stock market? Day traders? etc...<BR>If so, I have been thinking of getting involved a bit, and was wondering if anyone could recommend some good how to type books?<P>I want to do it my self, not through an investment counselor, as my retirement is all taken care of, this is just something I have always wanted to try.<BR>Thanks in advance||||reg||5
Z||000001||Formula||08-24-2000||08:56 AM||||I had an economics class last year and I invested about 70,000 in AMD right before they came out with the gigahertz processor, let me just say this, I won!!  I ended up in the low 100,000 mark in just 3 months, everyone else was only at like 80,000.  But I still want to be an electronics engineer.<P>btw.....the money was fake, dont ask me for any  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Formula_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||deceler8||08-24-2000||10:12 AM||||There are a zillion books out about this...I've read quite a few and they all basically list the same stuff.<P>Best way to do this is to take about 10 stocks and watch them for a while...get a feel for what the 52 week highs and lows are...after a while you'll see how much variation in the daily price occurs. What I do is trade on the volatility.<P>For about $20 per month you can subscribe to a real-time trading service (I use <A HREF="http://www.mytrack.com...you" TARGET=_blank>www.mytrack.com...you</A>  can download it for free). The $20 will get you live news feed, a chat service (you can chat live with other day-traders), charting and research.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||peterh||08-24-2000||01:03 PM||||I inherited some money 5 years ago. After paying off my ex-wife, getting re-married, and buying a car, I invested some money in Schwab mutual funds. The way I picked them was to pick funds with the highest increase over 5 or 10 years time. The funds have increased 2.5 times or approx 50% per year. I have taken some out to buy my Fiero for instance but I am basically interested in the long haul. The mutual funds are a large collection of like stocks, for example high tech. The makeup of each fund is managed by people who do nothing but research the stock market. This is just my experience and as they say your mileage may differ. Also do not invest anything that you need to live on as there are no guarantees. Hope this helps.<P><BR>||||reg||1
